{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/120003-the-st-s-power-and-that-power-limit-on-the-players","id":120003,"title":"The ST's power and that power limit on the players.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-04-26T15:40:46-07:00","id":120003,"title":"The ST's power and that power limit on the players.","content":"Hi all I wish to know this simple answer<br /> <br /> I have ST a few sessions I'm still a newb but i have read some of the important things in WOD before God machine.<br /> <br /> The concept is strate forward if the ST feels a rule is going to mess up the scenario.<br /> <br /> The ST can get rid of this or change it. However when this power infringes the players what rights do they have to object?<br /> <br /> The question is what are the players rights?<br /> <br /> Then how much of an antagonist should the ST be?<br /> <br /> Cruelty of ST should be sorted out when player are messing around right? <br /> <br /> Yet when it comes to that the adventure must go on and when a player has put in a lot of work to get their character <br /> were they want to be how do you punish them?<br /> <br /> I myself has have spent ours on Background then to have my character utterly destroyed by OP NPC's<br /> <br /> This action makes a player feel less involved but there still has to be challenge.<br /> <br /> The Game merchinics vs plot.<br /> <br /> The Player vs ST.<br /> <br /> What do you think is more important???","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2338-nagumyrehal","name":"nagumyrehal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2338_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-26T17:14:34-07:00","id":120046,"title":"","content":"I think you'd get more replies on the general world of darkness forums. However, it should never be The player vs the ST. If the game feels like a tug of war between the two than it's not really a game. Yes people will die and yes the party will lose at times but the St's goal is to create a game not a torture scenario. He should normally want you to win and if you don't it sucks but like every game you can't always win.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-26T20:47:03-07:00","id":120118,"title":"","content":"RPGs are usually at their best when the ST and players are all working together to have fun. If you are unhappy about something in the game have an out of character conversation with your ST about it. Be respectful, but he/she has probably put a lot of work into preparing and running the game for you. But make sure your feelings and concerns are heard. You guys should try to reach an understanding and compromise out of character.<br /> <br /> Despite it being called a &quot;game&quot; and having rules, it shouldn't be directly adversarial between you and the ST. Since the ST is basically god, because he can make the world and NPCs do anything. That means that he isn't trying to &quot;beat&quot; you. He should be trying to create a scenario that's challenging and fun for the players.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#800000\"><b>Demon Cat</b></span><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://flamingporcupine.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Illuminated Dark Ages character sheets and more</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10-demon-cat","name":"Demon Cat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"266"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-26T22:48:50-07:00","id":120182,"title":"","content":"Generally speaking, if a rule seems problematic to you for whatever reason, and you don't feel comfortable with just saying &quot;I'm setting/dumping/changing this rule for X reasons&quot;, discuss with your players, something like &quot;This rule seems problematic in these ways, I have these concerns about how it'll affect game balance/whatever, so I'm considering these revisions. What do you guys think?&quot;. I'm going to keep coming back to this, because it really is central.<br /> <br /> You do have final say as ST, but that doesn't mean that it's a good idea to just steamroll your players. If you disagree with what they say, just make clear that you have heard what they're saying, but that this is your judgement. If it doesn't work out, you can always change the rule later: most groups are pretty cool with revisions like this if they know they're a possibility ahead of time. (Also, documented fact: people like people who initially disagree with them but later change to agreement more than people who have always agreed. Not that that should be a factor, but I still think it's interesting.)<br /> <br /> <u><b>Always, always always work with your players</b></u>. Yes, there's a divide, and it's there for good reasons. But it's primarily a co-operative experience, and the best STs acknowledge and work with that as best they can. That doesn't mean always giving them their own way, of course, but generally speaking if people feel that you are listening and taking their viewpoint into account, they usually don't mind even if you go another way. Working with is not the same as agreeing with or being a doormat. Your job is to provide challenges to them and their characters in order for everyone to have fun, not to 'beat' them. A good paradigm to think about is if you were planning a party with them (like, a cake and games-party, not adventuring party). Being adversarial as an ST isn't ever productive. This can be a little confusing, because part of the job is to create adversaries for the PCs.<br /> <br /> One of the best things you can do is always be clear that you're open to discussion and suggestions. If players are saying, four or five times a session, that they don't feel they're relevant to the plot, or can meaningfully contribute, take that as a clue, and maybe adjust things a little. Don't just bitch about how the NPCs are totally legal builds and how it's all because they didn't optimise their characters right (I wouldn't think this is necessary advice, but I've personally experienced this, albeit very rarely).<br /> <br /> The worst STs I have played under have been didactorial and more concerned with their own fun than the players: as you said, flattening the players with NPCs who are way too powerful for the game they're running. Don't be that guy, nobody likes that ST, and unless your players are somehow captive (not literally, usually socially), you'd eventually run out of players because they'll find another way to have fun.<br /> <br /> With regard to your game mechanics vs plot, I'm heavily plot-favoured. Yes, the mechanics are necessary, and even kind of fun at times. But if there's a conflict, which there is some times, plot wins. If the exact wording of a power says something that doesn't make sense or conflicts with the flavour text, I'm usually OK with houseruling it to something more sensible/fun. Again, if you want to do this, discuss with your players.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-27T12:45:36-07:00","id":120467,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Allan53</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/120003-the-st-s-power-and-that-power-limit-on-the-players?p=120182#post120182\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Generally speaking, if a rule seems problematic to you for whatever reason, and you don't feel comfortable with just saying &quot;I'm setting/dumping/changing this rule for X reasons&quot;, discuss with your players, something like &quot;This rule seems problematic in these ways, I have these concerns about how it'll affect game balance/whatever, so I'm considering these revisions. What do you guys think?&quot;. I'm going to keep coming back to this, because it really is central.<br /> <br /> You do have final say as ST, but that doesn't mean that it's a good idea to just steamroll your players. If you disagree with what they say, just make clear that you have heard what they're saying, but that this is your judgement. If it doesn't work out, you can always change the rule later: most groups are pretty cool with revisions like this if they know they're a possibility ahead of time. (Also, documented fact: people like people who initially disagree with them but later change to agreement more than people who have always agreed. Not that that should be a factor, but I still think it's interesting.)<br /> <br /> <u><b>Always, always always work with your players</b></u>. Yes, there's a divide, and it's there for good reasons. But it's primarily a co-operative experience, and the best STs acknowledge and work with that as best they can. That doesn't mean always giving them their own way, of course, but generally speaking if people feel that you are listening and taking their viewpoint into account, they usually don't mind even if you go another way. Working with is not the same as agreeing with or being a doormat. Your job is to provide challenges to them and their characters in order for everyone to have fun, not to 'beat' them. A good paradigm to think about is if you were planning a party with them (like, a cake and games-party, not adventuring party). Being adversarial as an ST isn't ever productive. This can be a little confusing, because part of the job is to create adversaries for the PCs.<br /> <br /> One of the best things you can do is always be clear that you're open to discussion and suggestions. If players are saying, four or five times a session, that they don't feel they're relevant to the plot, or can meaningfully contribute, take that as a clue, and maybe adjust things a little. Don't just bitch about how the NPCs are totally legal builds and how it's all because they didn't optimise their characters right (I wouldn't think this is necessary advice, but I've personally experienced this, albeit very rarely).<br /> <br /> The worst STs I have played under have been didactorial and more concerned with their own fun than the players: as you said, flattening the players with NPCs who are way too powerful for the game they're running. Don't be that guy, nobody likes that ST, and unless your players are somehow captive (not literally, usually socially), you'd eventually run out of players because they'll find another way to have fun.<br /> <br /> With regard to your game mechanics vs plot, I'm heavily plot-favoured. Yes, the mechanics are necessary, and even kind of fun at times. But if there's a conflict, which there is some times, plot wins. If the exact wording of a power says something that doesn't make sense or conflicts with the flavour text, I'm usually OK with houseruling it to something more sensible/fun. Again, if you want to do this, discuss with your players.</div> </div> </div> </div>I see your point here and thanks you all for your reply I try and employ these Commandments to the games i'm in and see how they go. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2338-nagumyrehal","name":"nagumyrehal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2338_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}